direction: down

title: pipeline.build, stage by stage {
  near: top-center
  shape: text
  style.font-size: 24
}

example: |md
  Worked through `a,b,c`, with `b` hidden and `c` sorted descending.
  Every stage names its columns by position.
| {style.fill: "#1a1a2a"}

classes: {
  stage: {style.fill: "#4a4a2d"}
  shape_after: {style.fill: "#2d2d4a"}
  post: {style.fill: "#2d4a2d"}
}

source: |md
  ### The file
  `a,b,c`
| {class: shape_after}

select: |md
  ### `select 0,2`
  The carried columns: everything on
  display, then any column a sort key or a
  filter names, by their file positions.
  This is where a hidden column drops out,
  and where the columns take the order the
  user put them in.
| {class: stage}

enum: |md
  ### `enum -c id`
  Prepends the row id, the row's position in
  the file, which it keeps through every
  later stage.
| {class: stage}

fixed: |md
  `id,a,c`

  **The shape is now fixed.** The row id is
  at 0 and each carried column is at its
  place in the carried list, and every stage
  below reads those same numbers.
| {class: shape_after}

filter: |md
  ### `filter <expression>`
  Runs once the user has filtered. One
  moonblade expression, the filters ANDed,
  written by `expression.lua` and addressing
  columns as `col(2)`.

  This stage and the `select` and `enum`
  above it are `narrowing_stages`. `count`,
  `stats` and `frequency` open their own
  pipelines with the same three, so they
  report on the rows the user is looking at.
| {class: stage}

sort: |md
  ### `sort -s 2 -R`
  One stage per key, least significant first,
  because `xan sort` applies one direction to
  all its keys and is stable. `-N` numeric,
  `-R` descending.
| {class: stage}

slice: |md
  ### `slice -s 0 -l 1000`
  The page. Everything above decides which
  rows exist. Everything below is presentation.
| {class: stage}

display: |md
  ### `select -e ...`
  The row id, then one clause per displayed
  column: the printf spec or the detected
  decimal count, padding to the width the
  user pinned, a trailing space, and the
  header text to draw it under.

  **Column widths are applied here.** Every
  cell in a pinned column leaves at exactly
  that width, cut with an ellipsis if it ran
  long. The header is cut to the same width,
  because `view` sizes a column to its widest
  cell and the header is one of the cells.

  `try` wraps anything that can fail, so a
  value that fails to cast falls back to its
  raw text.
| {class: stage}

after_display: |md
  `id,a,c ▼`
| {class: shape_after}

view: |md
  ### `view -t table ...`
  `-e` draws every column at the width its
  content already has, which is the width the
  clause above gave it. Left to itself, `view`
  fits the table to the terminal.

  `-A` every row, `-M -I` drop the meta rows,
  `-r` right-aligns the numeric columns by
  position.
| {class: stage}

drawn: |md
  ### The drawn table
  ```
  ┌────┬────┬─────┐
  │ id │ a  │ c ▼ │
  ├────┼────┼─────┤
  │  0 │ 10 │   3 │
  │  2 │ 30 │   2 │
  └────┴────┴─────┘
  ```
| {class: shape_after}

parse: |md
  ### layout.parse
  Reads the row ids out of the first cell,
  then cuts that cell from every line. What
  the buffer holds starts at the first
  displayed column, and `statuscolumn.lua`
  draws the row number in the gutter.
| {class: post}

source -> select
select -> enum
enum -> fixed
fixed -> filter
filter -> sort
sort -> slice
slice -> display
display -> after_display
after_display -> view
view -> drawn
drawn -> parse
